  "body": "### Summary\n\n- Adds per-head entropy-based KV cache eviction that reduces memory usage by 2-5x with minimal quality loss (~96% exact match at 5x compression)\n- Includes a Python calibration script that profiles attention entropy per head, plus a pre-generated config for `bitnet_b1_58-large`\n- Minimal integration footprint: 4 lines of code to add to any llama.cpp-based inference loop\n\n### Motivation\n\nBitNet compresses weights to 1.58 bits, but KV cache activations remain in float16. For a 0.7B BitNet model, the KV cache is **68.6% of total memory** -- the dominant cost. This PR provides entropy-guided eviction that exploits BitNet's inherently focused attention patterns.\n\nKey insight: 49.8% of BitNet attention heads are sink/focused (entropy < 1.5 bits) and need very few KV cache entries. By allocating cache proportionally to head entropy, we compress aggressively with minimal quality loss.\n\n### Results\n\n| Compression | `--kv-budget` | Quality (exact match) | KV Memory Saved |\n|-------------|---------------|-----------------------|-----------------|\n| 2x          | 0.5           | ~96.5%                | 50%             |\n| 5x          | 0.2           | ~96%                  | 80%             |\n| 10x         | 0.1           | ~96%                  | 90%             |\n\nTotal memory savings for BitNet-0.7B at 2048 context: 34% at 2x, 55% at 5x, 62% at 10x.\n\n### How to Test\n\n```bash\n# 1. Calibrate (or use the included entropy_config.json)\npython calibrate_entropy.py --model 1bitLLM/bitnet_b1_58-large --output entropy_config.json\n\n# 2. Build (after adding CMake lines from README_ENTROPY_KV.md)\ncmake -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release\ncmake --build build --config Release\n\n# 3. Run with 2x compression\n./build/bin/llama-cli \\\n    -m models/bitnet_b1_58-large/ggml-model-i2_s.gguf \\\n    -p \"The meaning of life is\" -n 256 \\\n    --entropy-config entropy_config.json --kv-budget 0.5\n```\n\n### Files Added\n\n| File | Lines | Purpose |\n|------|-------|---------|\n| `entropy_kv_cache.h` | ~200 | Public C++ API header |\n| `entropy_kv_cache.cpp` | ~690 | Implementation (JSON parsing, budget computation, eviction) |\n| `calibrate_entropy.py` | ~430 | One-time Python calibration script |\n| `entropy_config.json` | ~420 | Pre-generated config for bitnet_b1_58-large (384 heads) |\n| `README_ENTROPY_KV.md` | ~195 | Usage guide and technical documentation |\n\n### Files Modified\n\nNone. This is a standalone addition. Integration requires:\n1. Adding a CMake target (3 lines in `CMakeLists.txt`)\n2. Adding 4 lines to the inference loop (`main.cpp` or `server.cpp`)\n\n### Design Notes\n\n- **Zero runtime overhead for entropy measurement** -- entropy profiles are pre-computed during calibration\n- **Position-level eviction** -- works within llama.cpp's flat KV cache (no restructuring needed)\n- **Always preserves the attention sink** (position 0) -- removing it crashes quality from 69% to 6.6%\n- **GQA support** -- handles grouped-query attention by taking max entropy across grouped query heads per KV head\n- **No external dependencies** -- includes minimal JSON parser to avoid adding a library dependency",
